---
/**
 * Personas — the human/agent split: what each side owns under metis.
 *
 * Copy is rendered verbatim from docs/copy.md §Personas (ADR-0003: the copy
 * deck is the single source of truth). Each copy string sits on ONE source
 * line and is never split by an inline element, so the built output greps
 * literally against the deck (convention from phase-1-ws-1.1).
 *
 * Layout: one column by default, two side by side from 768px up. Mobile-first
 * so the narrow layout needs no media query at all.
 *
 * Reveal (ADR-0005): the section opts into the page's shared observer with
 * `data-reveal`. The two columns carry `.reveal` individually rather than the
 * `.columns` grid wrapper carrying it once — a split is the point of this
 * section, so the halves arriving one after the other reads as the idea,
 * where fading the pair in as one block would not. Nothing sets a hidden base
 * state: the rules below render the finished section and the reveal is opt-in
 * motion layered on top (the never-invisible rule).
 */
---

<section id="personas" class="section" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="reveal">Two personas, one binary.</h2>

  <div class="columns">
    <div class="column reveal">
      <h3>The developer</h3>
      <p>You own the spec, the plan, and the priorities. metis gives you a ledger you can trust, review evidence you can audit, and a registry to run it across every project you own.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="column reveal">
      <h3>The agent</h3>
      <p>Agents get a printed protocol: one slice at a time, a brief committed before code, machine-checked scope, and a review they cannot perform on themselves.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .columns {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* 768px is a raw length on purpose: tokens.css owns colour, type and
     spacing, and deliberately defines no breakpoint scale. The value is the
     one the plan (§1.2) specifies for the two-column threshold. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .columns {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }
  }

  /* Raised surface gives each column its boundary; the hairline only sharpens
     an edge the fill already draws, so --color-border's decorative 1.40:1 is
     the right token here. Text pairings are in tokens.css's ratio table:
     text on surface 16.14:1 dark / 18.91:1 light, muted on surface 7.66:1
     dark / 6.06:1 light — both AA. */
  .column {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .column p {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    /* The grid track already sets the measure; --measure would over-constrain
       the column on wide viewports. */
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* --- Reveal stagger ----------------------------------------------------
     Choreography only: index.astro owns the animation, this component owns
     the order. The delays are inert custom properties until that rule reads
     them, which happens only once `is-revealed` lands and only outside
     reduced motion — so no media query is needed here.

     The h2 takes the 0ms default. Both columns then arrive left-to-right,
     the same direction the row is read in, 80ms apart. This deliberately
     ignores the 768px stack: at narrow widths the columns are already in
     document order top to bottom, and the same two delays read as the same
     sequence. */
  .column:first-child {
    --reveal-delay: 80ms;
  }

  .column:last-child {
    --reveal-delay: 160ms;
  }
</style>
